    "body": "<!-- relay-run-log -->\n**Relay run log** — issue #156\n\n- 2026-09-11 18:08Z — Relay picked up this issue for a BB worker run.\n- 2026-09-11 18:40Z — Relay BB run pushed an update. Pull request: #382 Tokens: 0 · Cost: $0.0000 Pushed branch `bb/relay-156-generic-rest-07-09-expose-transactiona-thr_mcbtwzskgn` to origin. ## Summary Implements issue #156: transactional item trash, restore, and the 30-day purge task. - **`DELETE /api/items/:id`**: soft-deletes an item and cascades to every inline database it owns and their rows, recursively, in one transaction. Idempotent on repeat calls, rejects the whole cascade if any database in the subtree is archived, and always returns the full authoritative row (never 204). - **`POST /api/items/:id/restore`**: symmetric restore of the same subtree, with the same idempotency and archived-database rejection semantics. - **30-day trash purge**: a new `graphile-worker` task (`itemTrashPurgeSweep`, daily at 03:45) permanently deletes items whose `deleted_at` is older than 30 days together with their cascade subtree, stopping at any branch that is live or trashed more recently than the cutoff. - Added `collectItemSubtree` (choke-point, BFS with cycle guard) and supporting store functions (`listDatabasesByParentItem`, `getAllItemsInDatabase`, `findItemsDeletedBefore`, `hardDeleteItem`, `getItemsByIdsIncludingDeleted`). - Full test coverage: choke-point cascade/idempotency/archived-rejection tests, HTTP-level route tests, and a dedicated purge-task test suite. `pnpm run verify` passes cleanly in both `backend/` and `web/`. Relay-Step-Status: pass\n- 2026-09-11 19:16Z — Relay BB run pushed an update. Pull request: #382 Tokens: 0 · Cost: $0.0000 Pushed the review-round fixes to `bb/relay-156-generic-rest-07-09-expose-transactiona-thr_mcbtwzskgn` (verified present on origin at `cae0a62`). ## Summary This round of work addresses the blocking AI code-review findings on the item trash/restore/purge implementation for issue #156: - **[High]** `restoreItem` no longer resurrects items that were independently trashed before a cascade delete touched their subtree: it now matches subtree rows against the cascade root's own `deletedAt` timestamp (stable for the whole transaction under Postgres's `now()` semantics), restoring only rows trashed together with the root. - **[Medium]** `restoreItem` now row-locks (`lockItemById`) before reading, closing a race where two concurrent restores of the same item could produce a spurious 404. - **[Medium]** `purgeExpiredTrash`'s in-memory purged-count bookkeeping is only updated after a subtree purge actually commits, so a rolled-back or rejected subtree is never mis-counted. - **[Low]** `findItemIncludingDeleted` no longer wraps its single-row read in an unnecessary transaction. - **[Acceptance criteria #12, #15]** The 30-day purge's hard-delete now goes through a new `chokePoint.purgeExpiredTrashSubtree` method — the same choke-point-guarded transaction, archived-database rejection, and subtree traversal pattern as `softDeleteItem`/`restoreItem` — instead of `purgeExpiredTrash.ts` calling `itemsStore` directly. Added regression tests covering the independently-trashed-row restore case, the concurrent-restore race, and purge respecting an archived database. Full `backend/` and `web/` `pnpm run verify` pipelines (lint, build, unit/integration/e2e tests) pass. Relay-Step-Status: pass\n- 2026-09-11 19:45Z — Relay BB run pushed an update. Pull request: #382 Tokens: 0 · Cost: $0.0000 Pushed to `bb/relay-156-generic-rest-07-09-expose-transactiona-thr_mcbtwzskgn` (verified present on origin at `feba0c9`). ## Summary This push addresses the last remaining blocking finding from PR #382's review (all 15 acceptance criteria were already met before this round): **[High] Purge/restore race destroying a freshly-restored item**: `purgeExpiredTrashSubtree`'s eligibility scan reads its candidate subtree with a plain, unlocked `SELECT`. A concurrent `restoreItem` call could commit between that scan and the delete loop, un-deleting a row just before the purge permanently removed it — silently destroying data the user had just restored. Fix: `itemsStore.hardDeleteItem` now guards its `DELETE` with `AND deleted_at IS NOT NULL`, so a race-restored row is skipped instead of destroyed, and it returns whether a row was actually removed so `purgeExpiredTrashSubtree`'s return value (and `purgeExpiredTrash`'s reported count) never counts a row the guard skipped. Also corrected a docstring that had overstated what the transactional re-check alone could guarantee against this race. Added a regression test exercising the guard directly against a concurrently-restored item. Full `backend/` and `web/` `pnpm run verify` pipelines pass (backend: 1050 integration tests + 5 e2e tests; web: 139 tests), all green. Relay-Step-Status: pass\n- 2026-09-11 19:58Z — This issue was resolved by #382, which just merged into develop.",
